1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,824
Breathe on a cold mirror and it fogs.

2
00:00:02,824 --> 00:00:04,628
A fish gulps water all day.

3
00:00:04,628 --> 00:00:06,716
An earthworm has no nose at all.

4
00:00:07,117 --> 00:00:09,095
All three are taking in oxygen.

5
00:00:09,095 --> 00:00:11,412
But each does it in a different way.

6
00:00:11,862 --> 00:00:14,465
Diya watched the fish
tank at her cousin's home.

7
00:00:14,465 --> 00:00:16,937
She asked: how does
each animal get oxygen?

8
00:00:16,937 --> 00:00:19,764
And where does the air go
after it enters my nose?

9
00:00:20,114 --> 00:00:21,878
Here is the short answer.

10
00:00:21,878 --> 00:00:25,044
Animals use their skin,
tubes, gills or lungs.

11
00:00:25,044 --> 00:00:29,699
In you, air travels down a branching
tree of tubes to tiny air sacs called

12
00:00:29,699 --> 00:00:30,599
alveoli.

13
00:00:30,895 --> 00:00:34,740
By the end, you will know the
respiratory organs of animals, the parts

14
00:00:34,740 --> 00:00:38,441
of the human respiratory system,
and the five steps of respiration.

15
00:00:39,091 --> 00:00:40,864
First, why do we breathe at all?

16
00:00:41,214 --> 00:00:44,317
Cells use oxygen to break
down food for energy.

17
00:00:44,317 --> 00:00:49,040
This makes carbon dioxide, which is
harmful, so it must leave the body.

18
00:00:49,490 --> 00:00:54,695
Taking in oxygen from the air and giving
out carbon dioxide is called breathing.

19
00:00:54,695 --> 00:00:58,049
In everyday words, it is
also called respiration.

20
00:00:58,699 --> 00:01:00,404
Let's zoom into your chest.

21
00:01:00,754 --> 00:01:05,501
Inside the chest sit two lungs,
one on each side, behind the ribs.

22
00:01:06,001 --> 00:01:07,269
Zoom into a lung.

23
00:01:07,269 --> 00:01:11,696
The tubes branch again and again, like
an upside-down tree, into very thin

24
00:01:11,696 --> 00:01:13,286
tubes called bronchioles.

25
00:01:13,786 --> 00:01:16,005
Zoom to the end of a bronchiole.

26
00:01:16,005 --> 00:01:19,213
It ends in many tiny,
thin-walled, bag-like sacs.

27
00:01:19,213 --> 00:01:20,768
These are the alveoli.

28
00:01:20,768 --> 00:01:23,643
They are wrapped in a
net of blood vessels.

29
00:01:24,293 --> 00:01:26,907
Now follow the five steps of respiration.

30
00:01:27,257 --> 00:01:28,157
Step one.

31
00:01:28,064 --> 00:01:30,414
Breathing, or pulmonary ventilation.

32
00:01:30,414 --> 00:01:34,417
Fresh air is drawn in, and air
with carbon dioxide goes out.

33
00:01:35,167 --> 00:01:36,067
Step two.

34
00:01:36,032 --> 00:01:40,747
Oxygen and carbon dioxide diffuse
across the thin wall of the alveoli.

35
00:01:40,747 --> 00:01:43,406
Diffuse means move from more to less.

36
00:01:44,156 --> 00:01:45,056
Step three.

37
00:01:44,871 --> 00:01:47,023
Blood carries these gases around the body.

38
00:01:47,773 --> 00:01:48,673
Step four.

39
00:01:48,647 --> 00:01:52,979
Oxygen and carbon dioxide diffuse
between the blood and the tissues.

40
00:01:53,729 --> 00:01:54,629
Step five.

41
00:01:54,511 --> 00:01:58,287
Cells use oxygen for their reactions,
and release carbon dioxide.

42
00:01:59,037 --> 00:02:00,054
Remember this.

43
00:02:00,054 --> 00:02:02,497
Gases are swapped only in the alveoli.

44
00:02:03,097 --> 00:02:04,970
Now trace the path of the air.

45
00:02:05,320 --> 00:02:08,828
Air enters the external
nostrils and the nasal chamber.

46
00:02:08,828 --> 00:02:12,297
Then the pharynx, a passage
shared by food and air.

47
00:02:12,297 --> 00:02:14,745
Next is the larynx, the sound box.

48
00:02:15,195 --> 00:02:19,498
A flap called the epiglottis covers
the glottis when we swallow, so food

49
00:02:19,498 --> 00:02:20,398
stays out.

50
00:02:20,324 --> 00:02:24,770
Then air goes down the trachea, which
splits into right and left bronchi,

51
00:02:24,770 --> 00:02:27,439
then bronchioles, and finally the alveoli.

52
00:02:28,089 --> 00:02:30,199
These parts do two different jobs.

53
00:02:30,549 --> 00:02:34,234
The conducting part runs from the
nostrils to the terminal bronchioles.

54
00:02:34,234 --> 00:02:38,233
It carries the air, cleans out dust,
warms it to body temperature and makes

55
00:02:38,233 --> 00:02:39,133
it moist.

56
00:02:39,361 --> 00:02:42,747
The exchange part is the
alveoli and their ducts.

57
00:02:42,747 --> 00:02:47,200
Here oxygen and carbon dioxide
actually pass between air and blood.

58
00:02:47,850 --> 00:02:49,421
Now, where do the lungs sit?

59
00:02:49,771 --> 00:02:53,029
Each lung is covered by a
double layer called the pleura.

60
00:02:53,029 --> 00:02:56,366
Fluid between the layers reduces
friction as the lungs move.

61
00:02:56,816 --> 00:03:00,562
The lungs sit in the thoracic
chamber, an air-tight box.

62
00:03:00,562 --> 00:03:05,413
The backbone is behind, the sternum
in front, the ribs on the sides, and

63
00:03:05,413 --> 00:03:08,359
a dome-shaped muscle,
the diaphragm, below.

64
00:03:08,959 --> 00:03:09,859
Your turn.

65
00:03:09,775 --> 00:03:12,698
The trachea, bronchi and
bronchioles carry air.

66
00:03:12,698 --> 00:03:14,479
Do they exchange gases too?

67
00:03:14,479 --> 00:03:15,678
Pause and decide.

68
00:03:18,878 --> 00:03:19,778
No!

69
00:03:19,319 --> 00:03:21,092
They only conduct the air.

70
00:03:21,092 --> 00:03:23,699
Gas exchange happens only in the alveoli.

71
00:03:24,349 --> 00:03:25,907
Now, a worked example.

72
00:03:25,907 --> 00:03:27,980
Animals breathe in different ways.

73
00:03:27,980 --> 00:03:29,925
Which organ does each one use?

74
00:03:30,275 --> 00:03:34,432
Simple animals like sponges and
flatworms just use their body surface.

75
00:03:34,432 --> 00:03:37,920
An earthworm uses its moist
cuticle, its thin wet skin.

76
00:03:38,370 --> 00:03:42,479
Insects have a network of tiny tubes,
called tracheal tubes, that carry

77
00:03:42,479 --> 00:03:43,834
air inside the body.

78
00:03:44,284 --> 00:03:46,415
Fishes and many water animals use gills.

79
00:03:46,415 --> 00:03:48,499
Reptiles, birds and mammals use lungs.

80
00:03:48,499 --> 00:03:51,820
Frogs use lungs, and can also
breathe through their moist skin.

81
00:03:52,470 --> 00:03:53,931
Now, the exam trap.

82
00:03:54,281 --> 00:03:58,097
Many students write that the trachea
has complete rings of cartilage.

83
00:03:58,497 --> 00:04:00,185
But the rings are incomplete.

84
00:04:00,185 --> 00:04:04,084
They support the trachea, the bronchi
and the first bronchioles, and keep

85
00:04:04,084 --> 00:04:04,984
them open.

86
00:04:05,469 --> 00:04:06,642
Where is this asked?

87
00:04:06,642 --> 00:04:10,194
As match questions on animals and their
respiratory organs, and statements

88
00:04:10,194 --> 00:04:12,021
on the parts of the human system.

89
00:04:12,621 --> 00:04:13,848
Exam level now.

90
00:04:13,848 --> 00:04:16,886
Match each animal with
its respiratory organ.

91
00:04:16,886 --> 00:04:18,165
Pause and solve.

92
00:04:23,565 --> 00:04:24,950
The answer is D.

93
00:04:25,450 --> 00:04:27,941
The earthworm uses its moist cuticle.

94
00:04:27,941 --> 00:04:31,151
The cockroach, an insect,
uses tracheal tubes.

95
00:04:31,651 --> 00:04:35,707
The fish uses gills, and the
pigeon, a bird, uses lungs.

96
00:04:36,307 --> 00:04:37,847
Here is your recap.

97
00:04:37,847 --> 00:04:39,430
Screenshot this card.

98
00:04:42,630 --> 00:04:44,816
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

99
00:04:45,216 --> 00:04:46,116
One.

100
00:04:45,644 --> 00:04:47,407
Where does gas exchange happen?

101
00:04:48,307 --> 00:04:49,207
Two.

102
00:04:48,743 --> 00:04:50,674
What does the conducting part do?

103
00:04:51,574 --> 00:04:52,474
Three.

104
00:04:52,095 --> 00:04:54,636
What forms the floor of
the thoracic chamber?

105
00:04:55,736 --> 00:05:00,058
Next time: what makes air rush into
your lungs, and how much air can they

106
00:05:00,058 --> 00:05:00,958
really hold?

107
00:05:00,931 --> 00:05:04,488
We find out in mechanism of
breathing and respiratory volumes.
